.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH murk-add 1  "add " 
.SH NAME
murk\-add \- Add or update a secret
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmurk add\fR [\fB\-\-desc\fR] [\fB\-\-example\fR] [\fB\-\-group\fR] [\fB\-\-tag\fR] [\fB\-\-vault\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIKEY\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Add or update a secret
.PP
On a terminal, murk asks for the value with a hidden prompt. When stdin is piped, murk reads all of it as the value and drops one trailing newline.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-desc\fR \fI<DESC>\fR
Description of the secret
.TP
\fB\-\-example\fR \fI<EXAMPLE>\fR
Example value
.TP
\fB\-\-group\fR \fI<GROUP>\fR
Who can read the secret: `everyone`, `me` (a private value for you only) or a group name (experimental). Omit it to keep an existing secret\*(Aqs tier. A new secret is shared with everyone
.TP
\fB\-\-tag\fR \fI<TAG>\fR
Tag for grouping (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-\-vault\fR \fI<VAULT>\fR [default: .murk]
Vault filename
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fIKEY\fR>
Name of the secret
